<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=incandescent-light-bulb">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Incandescent light bulb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as incandescent lamp, incandescent light globe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An incandescent light bulb is an electric light in which current heats a filament until it glows inside an evacuated or gas-filled glass bulb; Humphry Davy made an electric incandescent light in 1802 at the Royal Institution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Many experimenters followed, and from 1878 Joseph Swan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Thomas Edison developed practical carbon-filament bulbs: Edison's first successful test, on 22 October 1879, lasted 13.5 hours, and Swan's bulbs were in daily use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1881. Tungsten filaments, patented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1904, gave brighter, longer-lasting light.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/incandescent-light-bulb.jpg" alt="Incandescent light bulb" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terren · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edison_Carbon_Bulb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1802</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>London</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Humphry Davy · Joseph Swan · Thomas Edison</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Credit for the first practical bulb is disputed between Thomas Edison, whose first successful test was in 1879, and Joseph Swan, whose bulbs were in use in London by 1881.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=incandescent-light-bulb">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
